    slug: 'class-vs-instance',
    title: 'Shared versus per-object data',
    subtitle: 'Class attributes',
    concept:
      'An attribute assigned directly in the class body is a **class attribute**: one copy shared by every instance. An attribute assigned via `self` in `__init__` is an **instance attribute**: each object gets its own.\n\nUse class attributes for constants and shared counters, instance attributes for anything describing one particular object.\n\nThe common trap: a mutable class attribute like a list is shared, so appending through one instance affects all of them.',
    example: 'class Dog:\n    species = "Canis familiaris"\n    count = 0\n\n    def __init__(self, name):\n        self.name = name\n        Dog.count += 1\n\na = Dog("Rex")\nb = Dog("Fido")\n\nprint(a.species, b.species)\nprint(Dog.count)\nprint(a.name, b.name)',
