    concept:
      'An **exception** is raised when an operation cannot proceed \u2014 dividing by zero, converting `"abc"` to an int, reading a missing dict key. Left alone it stops the program.\n\n`try` runs risky code; `except` catches a failure and decides what to do. Catch **specific** types (`ValueError`, `ZeroDivisionError`) rather than everything, so real bugs are not silently hidden.\n\n`else` runs when no exception occurred, and `finally` always runs \u2014 ideal for cleanup.',
    example: 'def safe_divide(a, b):\n    try:\n        result = a / b\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        return "undefined"\n    else:\n        return result\n\nprint(safe_divide(10, 2))\nprint(safe_divide(10, 0))\n\ntry:\n    n = int("abc")\nexcept ValueError as err:\n    print("failed:", err)\nfinally:\n    print("always runs")',
    note: '`as err` captures the exception object so you can inspect its message.',
