      '`raise` signals that a function cannot do what was asked. It is better than returning `None` or `-1`, because the caller cannot accidentally ignore it.\n\nChoose the type that fits: `ValueError` for a bad value, `TypeError` for a wrong type. For domain-specific problems, define your own by subclassing `Exception` \u2014 the class body can be just `pass`.\n\nA custom exception type lets callers catch exactly your error and nothing else.',
    example: 'class TooColdError(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef check_temperature(t):\n    if not isinstance(t, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError("temperature must be numeric")\n    if t < -50:\n        raise TooColdError(f"{t} is implausible")\n    return f"{t} is fine"\n\nprint(check_temperature(20))\n\nfor value in [-100, "hot"]:\n    try:\n        print(check_temperature(value))\n    except TooColdError as e:\n        print("too cold:", e)\n    except TypeError as e:\n        print("bad type:", e)',
